  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nAs of 2am, a decaying storm is exiting far south central SD with \nadditional convection moving in over north central SD. Current temps \nrange in the upper 50s to the upper 60s and winds light. By 12Z \nmodels have a good handle on a positive tilted trough over ND \nextending northeastward to a stronger surface low, and its upper \nlow, over the Hudson Bay area. On the southern extent of this \ntrough, the weaker surface low is forecast to be over Canada/ND \nborder with its weak warm front extending through central SD as the \nhigh pressure system that was overhead continues to shift off \ntowards the southeast. By this afternoon and into early this \nevening, the center of the low will track southeast and over \nND/SD/MN border (00Z) with the warm front turned surface trough \nextending southwestward through northeastern SD into western NE (to \na weak surface low here) and its cold front draped across ND/SD \nborder. Late afternoon through this evening is when we start to \nreally see an increase in shortwave activity and an increase in the \n500mb winds, between 40-55kts, with the CWA on the southern extent \nof this UL system in Canada. \n\nLatest run of the HREF ensemble mean indicates the potential for \nspotty convection over portions of north central through \nnortheastern SD/MN into ND this morning through the early afternoon, \nhowever, there are differences in timing and exact location of this \nwith some of the CAMs not showing much at all due to a slight cap \ntill about 15Z. So low confidence on outcome and therefore stuck \nmainly with the NBM. We will see the potential in increased activity \naround ~20Z where scattered showers and thunderstorms are forecast \nto fire up (northeast to southwest over the CWA) with the convection \nincreasing in coverage (and potential strength) along and near the \nsurface trough from south central through northeastern SD/MN through \nthe early evening. Both HREF/NBM locks into the bulk of the precip \nand pops over northeastern SD/MN during this time. Beyond ~02Z, the \ntrough will be more to our southeast with the bulk of the storms \nover southern to southeastern SD into MN. Individual CAMs do differ \non storm mode whether its more of cells merging into clusters \n(FV3/HRRR/NAMNEST) or more of a line of clusters (HRW NSSL). HRRR \nand NAMNest also have cells developing or moving in over south \ncentral SD from the Black Hills. So overall low confidence in exact \nstorm type and location and which scenario will play out so again \nwent with the NBM for now but added in 20% pops over central SD \nwhere NBM had none. Closer to time, HREF or Conshort can be added to \nthe pop grids.\n\nSurface dewpoints will range in the 60s to potentially lower 70s \nover the eastern CWA with highs overall in the 80s creating warm and \nmoist conditions at the lower levels. The greatest instability of \n1000-2000j/kg of CAPE will be over the eastern half of SD into MN \nwith the highest values over southeastern SD into NE by the evening \ntime with potential max values above 3000 j/kg per HREF. RAP is a \nbit higher on the instability between 2000-3000j/kg of ML/MUCAPE \nwith CAPE decreasing with sunset. Wind shear will range between 30-\n40kts with an increase in shear to 50kts+ after 00Z. Mid level lapse \nrates will also range between 6-7C/km. Therefore, any storm has the \npotential to become severe especially over the eastern half of SD \nwhere the higher shear and instability lies, especially along the \nnortheast to southwest oriented trough. With the bulk shear and \nsteep lapse rates supercells will be able to form along with the \nclusters as mentioned. If they do form, splitting supercells are \npossible given the long and straight hodographs (especially over \nNESD/MN where the better overlap of Cape/Shear). SPC did add a 2% \ntornado risk south of a line from Murdo to Watertown, however, as \nmentioned hodographs seem to lack low level curvature with 0-1KM SRH \naround 100m2s2 or lower, 0-1km shear below 15kts, and STP below\n1.The greatest potential looks to be more south of the CWA. \nHowever, you cannot rule it out especially when the atmosphere \nhas the potential to produce supercells along the boundary and \nlower LCLs. So due to the severe threat, the SPC expanded the \nSlight Risk (level 2/5) and now is along and east of a line from\nMurdo to Sisseton where the potential for larger hail (1-2\") is\npossible and wind gusts of 60-70 mph. There is a Marginal Risk \n(level 1/5) along and west of a line from Sisseton to Murdo to a\nline from Eagle Butte to Eureka with the potential of quarter \nsize hail and wind gusts of 60mph. Brief heavy downpours are \nalso possible in both the marginal and slight risk areas.\n\nThe cold front will track a bit southward overnight and extend \nhorizontally across central through east central SD by 12Z before \nstalling out. Additional chances of showers and thunderstorms are \npossible tonight/overnight as CAMs suggest more isolated or clustery \nstorms passi",
